---
title: Microwave jacket potatoes
description: Lesson 8.4. How a microwave heats, and what the power button really does.
servings: 2
time: 30 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, mastering-the-machines]
lesson: "8.4"
technique: microwave cooking
status: draft
---

> Technique: microwave cooking. A microwave heats the outside of food
> fast and relies on that heat travelling inwards. Good results come
> from giving the heat time to travel: lower power, a turn halfway,
> and a rest at the end.

Scrub @potatoes{2}(floury, about 250 g each) and prick each one all over with a fork. Put them on a #microwave-safe plate{}.

Microwave at 50% power for ~{8%minutes}. Turn the potatoes over and microwave at 50% power for ~{8%minutes} more, until a knife goes into the middle with no resistance. Times are for an oven of about 800 W; yours will differ.

> Science: a microwave oven has a magnetron that emits electromagnetic
> waves. The waves act on water molecules and, less strongly, on oil,
> and they reach only the outer 0.5 to 2.5 cm of the food. Everything
> deeper is heated by conduction from that outer layer, which is slow.
> Look up: magnetron, dielectric heating, thermal conduction.
-- source: Lan Lam, "Microwave Cooking: When to Use the Power Levels", America's Test Kitchen, 2019-07-25, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1843-microwave-cooking-when-to-use-the-power-levels ("only the outer ¼ to 1 inch of food") ; Kristin Sargianis, "Your Microwave Questions Answered", America's Test Kitchen, 2019-06-17, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1762-your-microwave-questions-answered

> Science: the power setting does not make the waves weaker. The
> magnetron can only be on or off, and a lower setting switches it off
> for part of the time. In a test with LED bulbs in place of food, the
> bulb was lit for 30 seconds of each minute at 50% power, 18 seconds
> at 30% and 6 seconds at 10%. Look up: duty cycle.
-- source: Lan Lam, "Microwave Cooking: When to Use the Power Levels", America's Test Kitchen, 2019-07-25, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1843-microwave-cooking-when-to-use-the-power-levels

> Why half power for a potato: something thick that cannot be stirred
> needs time for the heat to move from the surface to the centre. At
> full power the outside overcooks while the middle is still hard.
-- source: Lan Lam, "Microwave Cooking: When to Use the Power Levels", America's Test Kitchen, 2019-07-25, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1843-microwave-cooking-when-to-use-the-power-levels

Cover the potatoes with an upturned bowl and leave them for ~{5%minutes}.

> Why rest, and why cover: resting lets the hot and cool spots even
> out by conduction, and the cover traps steam, which carries on the
> cooking.
-- source: Kristin Sargianis, "Your Microwave Questions Answered", America's Test Kitchen, 2019-06-17, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1762-your-microwave-questions-answered

Cut a cross in the top of each potato, squeeze it open, and add @butter{30%g} and @salt{1/2%tsp}.

> Next time, without the recipe: for anything thick, use half power,
> turn or stir it halfway, and let it stand covered before you judge
> it. For liquids and thin things, full power is fine.
